# D51 / `todo/t0718` — `unify`'s smart-pointer coercion is keyed on the TYPED
# builtin IDENTITY (`DefInfo.builtin_kind`), never on the definition's NAME.
#
# THE CELL AT THE INTERSECTION OF TWO AXES THE SET COVERED SEPARATELY:
# declaration form `enum` (covered for arm A only) x `unify` arm B (covered
# for `struct` only). It was CHANGED and pinned by nothing — found by
# regenerating the whole product with
# `scripts/coercion_identity_matrix.py` instead of enumerating by hand.
#
# A USER `enum RWLock[T]` is not the builtin `RWLock`: it gets its own `DefId`
# with `builtin_kind = None`, so it is not transparent for coercion and
# handing it to an `int` parameter is a type error.
#
# ⛔ BEFORE THE TYPED FLAG THIS COMPILED — `gg check` OK, then the variant
# payload's storage was read as an `int`. Same accept-then-miscompile class
# as its `struct` sibling `user_rwlock_struct_ret.gg`, which is what makes
# this a row rather than a note.
#
# Asserted by `coercion_identity_user_rwlock_enum_ret_rejects`.

enum RWLock[T]:
    Held(T)

int take_int(int x):
    return x + 1

void main():
    RWLock[int] w = RWLock.Held(7)
    print(take_int(w))
